# Example .bitbucket/CODEOWNERS — copy to .bitbucket/CODEOWNERS at the repo root.
#
# Bitbucket code owners are ADVISORY: this file chooses who is added as a
# reviewer on PRs touching the listed paths, but it never gates a merge (unlike
# GitHub and GitLab, where code-owner approval is enforceable). The hard stop on
# Bitbucket is branch restrictions plus a merge check — see the trust block at
# the top of examples/bitbucket-pipelines.yml and the "Trust" section of
# docs/ci/bitbucket.md.
#
# Designating approvers: the `lock-owners` team below is defined in
# examples/bitbucket-teams.yaml — copy that to .bitbucket/teams.yaml (same
# directory) and edit its `contributors` to add or remove authorized
# approvers. No workspace-admin rights needed. (Alternative: reference a
# workspace group as @workspace-slug/group-slug if you manage groups in the
# workspace UI.)
#
# Select reviewers with a strategy suffix on the LAST owner of a line:
#   :all            ask every lock owner (strongest)
#   :random(N)      ask N random members
#   :least_busy(N)  ask the N members with the fewest open reviews
# With no suffix a group/team picks one member at random.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the last matching pattern wins.
.pocket-zipper/** @teams/lock-owners:all

# Who can edit these two files (.bitbucket/CODEOWNERS and .bitbucket/teams.yaml)
# can rewire who is asked to review, so keep .bitbucket/* under the same
# ownership. `.bitbucket/*` matches files directly under .bitbucket/ and is the
# last rule, so it wins for those paths.
.bitbucket/* @teams/lock-owners:all